  "rules": [
    "When sentiment is consistently zero (0.00) across the history window, the model exhibits a strong upward bias and fails to capture immediate volatility spikes; correct by applying a negative mean reversion factor of -5% to -10% on the forecasted path.",
    "If the true outcome shows a sharp spike followed by an extended period of decline (e.g., Example 2, 3, 4), the model's linear upward drift significantly overshoots the subsequent trough; correct by reducing the forecast slope by 60-70% once a volatility spike is detected in the first 5 days.",
    "The model systematically underestimates downside risk during periods of mixed sentiment (values between 0.20 and 1.00) where historical data shows high variance; apply a floor correction to prevent forecasts from drifting below the lowest 30-day historical low by at least 4%.",
    "When the forecasted path remains flat or slowly rising while history shows a clear downtrend, ignore the sentiment signal entirely and revert to a mean-reversion strategy targeting the average of the last 18 days minus 2 standard deviations."
  ]
}
